  <section>
    <h2>Problem Statement</h2>
    <p>Poor call quality was often found only after users complained. The network team relied on fixed rules, such as "packet loss above X%", that missed many bad calls and raised false alarms. With thousands of cells in the network, engineers had no objective way to decide which cells to inspect first. The team needed a data-driven way to predict poor-quality calls early and point engineers to the cells that caused the most impact.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Solution Approach</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Data collection &amp; cleaning:</strong> Gathered call detail and network data from <span class="est">3</span> sources. Removed duplicates, fixed invalid values and handled missing data to keep the dataset complete and reliable.</li>
      <li><strong>Label creation:</strong> Turned the MOS value into a binary target: 0 for MOS ≥ 3 (good) and 1 for MOS &lt; 3 (needs inspection).</li>
      <li><strong>Exploratory data analysis:</strong> Used visual analysis to find patterns. For example, poor quality rose sharply at peak hours and above <span class="est">~2%</span> packet loss.</li>
      <li><strong>Feature engineering:</strong> Built <span class="est">40+</span> features, such as jitter-to-latency ratios, peak-hour flags, rolling cell-level averages and grouped device and codec categories. Selected the <span class="est">25</span> strongest features.</li>
      <li><strong>Modeling:</strong> Compared Logistic Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest and Gradient Boosting. Chose <span class="est">Random Forest</span> for the best balance of recall, precision and interpretability.</li>
      <li><strong>Imbalance &amp; threshold tuning:</strong> Used class weights and tuned the decision threshold to catch more poor calls while keeping false alarms low.</li>
      <li><strong>Evaluation:</strong> Measured ROC-AUC, precision, recall, F1 and the confusion matrix on a held-out test set. Focused on recall for label 1, because a missed poor call costs more than a false alarm.</li>
      <li><strong>Cell-level aggregation &amp; dashboard:</strong> Joined each scored call to its serving cell_id. Counted label 1 calls per cell and ranked cells from highest to lowest. Showed the <span class="est">poor-call rate (% of label 1 calls)</span> next to the count, so small cells with severe faults were not hidden by busy cells.</li>
      <li><strong>Deployment &amp; monitoring:</strong> Deployed on Azure with daily batch scoring and a daily dashboard refresh. Set up drift and performance checks and <span class="est">monthly</span> retraining when needed.</li>
      <li><strong>Documentation:</strong> Wrote reports on the methods, findings and recommendations for technical and business stakeholders.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Results &amp; Business Impact</h2>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Metric</th><th>Result</th><th>Business meaning</th></tr>
      <tr><td>ROC-AUC</td><td><strong><span class="est">0.92</span></strong></td><td>Separates good and poor calls very well</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Recall (label 1)</td><td><strong><span class="est">84%</span></strong> (rule-based baseline: <span class="est">~55%</span>)</td><td>Catches most poor-quality calls</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Precision (label 1)</td><td><strong><span class="est">78%</span></strong></td><td>About 4 of every 5 flagged calls really need inspection</td></tr>
      <tr><td>F1 score (label 1)</td><td><strong><span class="est">0.81</span></strong></td><td>Good balance of detection and false alarms</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Overall accuracy</td><td><strong><span class="est">~95%</span></strong></td><td>Reliable across all calls</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cell concentration</td><td><strong>Top <span class="est">10%</span></strong> of cells held <span class="est">~60%</span> of label 1 calls</td><td>Fixing a small set of cells solves most of the problem</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Customer complaints</td><td><strong><span class="est">~18%</span></strong> reduction</td><td>Issues fixed before users noticed</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Time to detect issues</td><td><strong><span class="est">~1 day → ~2 hours</span></strong></td><td>Faster fixes and less user impact</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Manual inspection effort</td><td><strong><span class="est">~35%</span></strong> reduction</td><td>Engineers inspect ranked cells, not random samples</td></tr>
    </table>

    <h3>Business Value</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>From reactive to proactive:</strong> Finds poor-quality calls early, before complaints come in.</li>
      <li><strong>Action at the right level:</strong> Cell-level ranking turns call predictions into a clear list of network locations to fix.</li>
      <li><strong>Better use of engineers:</strong> Focuses field and network work on the cells with the highest user impact.</li>
      <li><strong>Stronger SLA compliance:</strong> Earlier fixes help meet service quality targets.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear root causes:</strong> Feature drivers show why quality drops, which guides network changes.</li>
      <li><strong>Stays accurate:</strong> Monitoring and retraining keep the model reliable as the network changes.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Challenges</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Class imbalance:</strong> Poor calls were a small share of traffic. Used class weights and threshold tuning instead of relying on accuracy alone.</li>
      <li><strong>Data quality:</strong> Call records had missing values and inconsistent fields across sources. Built cleaning and validation steps before modeling.</li>
      <li><strong>Call-to-cell mapping:</strong> Some calls had missing or changed cell_ids because of handovers between cells. Mapped each call to its primary serving cell and excluded records with no valid cell_id.</li>
      <li><strong>Volume bias in ranking:</strong> Busy cells naturally have more label 1 calls. Showed the poor-call rate with the count, so engineers could see both user impact and fault severity.</li>
      <li><strong>Many related network factors:</strong> Jitter, latency and packet loss move together. Removed highly correlated features to keep the model stable and easy to explain.</li>
      <li><strong>Threshold choice:</strong> Too low a threshold caused alarm fatigue; too high missed bad calls. Agreed the final threshold with the network team.</li>
      <li><strong>Data drift:</strong> Network upgrades and new devices changed the data over time. Added drift checks and scheduled retraining.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Positioning: Where the Model Fits in the Business</h2>

    <h3>Business Context</h3>
    <p>Voice call quality directly affects customer satisfaction and SLA commitments. The network operations team manages thousands of cells, but cannot inspect each one. The model and its cell-ranking dashboard sit between raw call data and the operations team as an early-warning layer that tells engineers which cells to fix first.</p>

    <h3>Place in the Network Operations Workflow</h3>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Step</th><th>What happens</th><th>Model's role</th></tr>
      <tr><td>1. Call data capture</td><td>Network systems log call and quality metrics with the serving cell_id</td><td>Source of model features</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2. Data storage</td><td>Records load into Azure storage and SQL tables</td><td>Daily feature build</td></tr>
      <tr class="highlight"><td>3. Quality prediction</td><td>Model scores each call</td><td>Gives probability and 0/1 label</td></tr>
      <tr class="highlight"><td>4. Cell ranking</td><td>Label 1 calls are grouped by cell_id and ranked from highest to lowest count</td><td>Sets the inspection priority by cell</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5. Inspection</td><td>Engineers check the top-ranked cells and their peak time windows</td><td>Feature drivers point to the likely cause</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6. Network action</td><td>Capacity, routing, antenna or configuration changes</td><td>Fixes issues before more users are affected</td></tr>
      <tr><td>7. Feedback</td><td>New MOS results and inspection outcomes are logged</td><td>Confirms the fix and feeds retraining</td></tr>
    </table>

    <h3>Who Uses the Output</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Network operations engineers:</strong> Get a ranked list of cells to inspect, with the likely causes.</li>
      <li><strong>Operations managers:</strong> Track cell-level quality trends and plan field team capacity.</li>
      <li><strong>Customer service teams:</strong> Get early notice of areas likely to raise complaints.</li>
      <li><strong>Leadership:</strong> Get a clearer view of service quality and SLA risk.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="guardrail">
      <strong>Guardrails:</strong> The model is an early-warning tool, not a replacement for network diagnostics. A high cell rank means "inspect this cell first." Engineers confirm the root cause before they change the network.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Future Work</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Move from daily batch scoring to near real-time scoring for faster alerts.</li>
      <li>Rank cells with a combined score of poor-call count and rate, weighted by user impact.</li>
      <li>Add more data sources, such as weather, network events and device firmware versions.</li>
      <li>Test time-series models to predict cell-level quality drops before they happen.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

